============================================================
THE RAPIDITY MONOID
============================================================

  The set {1/(2n+1) : n >= 1} under (+) is NOT closed.
  But the SUBSET of compositions that ARE closed forms a structure.

  The closure condition c = ab/(a+b+1) being integer means
  the rapidity-closed triples form a PARTIAL BINARY OPERATION.

  Is it associative? Check: (1/5 (+) 1/7) (+) 1/9 = 1/3 (+) 1/9 = 3/7.
  And: 1/5 (+) (1/7 (+) 1/9) = 1/5 (+) 1/4 = ?
  1/7 (+) 1/9 = 0.2500000000 = 1/4 = 0.2500000000? True
  1/5 (+) 1/4 = 0.4285714286
  = 3/7 = 0.428571

  1/5 (+) 1/4 = 9/19. Is 19 odd? YES. Is (19-1)/2 = 9 an integer? YES.
  So 1/5 (+) 1/4 = 9/19 = 1/(19/9)... not of form 1/(2c+1).
  BUT 1/4 is NOT of the form 1/(2n+1). It's 1/4 with 4 even.

  The rapidity composition IS associative (it's velocity addition).
  But the SET {1/(2n+1)} is not closed under it.
  The closed triples are scattered through the space.

============================================================
GRAPH STRUCTURE OF RAPIDITY COMPOSITIONS
============================================================

  Draw a directed graph: edge a -> c if there exists b with
  1/(2a+1) (+) 1/(2b+1) = 1/(2c+1) and a <= b.
  (So a is always the smaller component.)

  Reachability from each node (up to a=15):
    a= 2 (1/  5) -> 1(1/3)
    a= 3 (1/  7) -> 1(1/3), 2(1/5)
    a= 4 (1/  9) -> 2(1/5), 3(1/7)
    a= 5 (1/ 11) -> 2(1/5), 3(1/7), 4(1/9)
    a= 6 (1/ 13) -> 3(1/7), 4(1/9), 5(1/11)
    a= 7 (1/ 15) -> 3(1/7), 5(1/11), 6(1/13)
    a= 8 (1/ 17) -> 2(1/5), 4(1/9), 5(1/11), 6(1/13), 7(1/15)
    a= 9 (1/ 19) -> 3(1/7), 4(1/9), 6(1/13), 7(1/15), 8(1/17)
    a=10 (1/ 21) -> 5(1/11), 8(1/17), 9(1/19)
    a=11 (1/ 23) -> 5(1/11), 7(1/15), 8(1/17), 9(1/19), 10(1/21)
    a=12 (1/ 25) -> 6(1/13), 8(1/17), 9(1/19), 10(1/21), 11(1/23)
    a=13 (1/ 27) -> 6(1/13), 11(1/23), 12(1/25)
    a=14 (1/ 29) -> 4(1/9), 7(1/15), 8(1/17), 9(1/19), 11(1/23), 12(1/25), 13(1/27)
    a=15 (1/ 31) -> 3(1/7), 5(1/11), 7(1/15), 9(1/19), 10(1/21), 11(1/23), 12(1/25), 13(1/27)

============================================================
FORBIDDEN COMPOSITION: CAN WE REACH 7 AND 21?
============================================================

  To compose to c=3 (i.e., 1/7 = the fourth):
  Need (a-3)(b-3) = 12.
  12 = 1*12 = 2*6 = 3*4
  (a,b) = (4,15), (5,9), (6,7)
    1/9 (+) 1/31 = 1/7: c = 3
    1/11 (+) 1/19 = 1/7: c = 3
    1/13 (+) 1/15 = 1/7: c = 3

  To compose to c=10 (i.e., 1/21):
  Need (a-10)(b-10) = 110.
  110 = 1*110 = 2*55 = 5*22 = 10*11
    1/23 (+) 1/241 = 1/21: c = 10
    1/25 (+) 1/131 = 1/21: c = 10
    1/31 (+) 1/65 = 1/21: c = 10
    1/41 (+) 1/43 = 1/21: c = 10

  GENERAL: c = ab/(a+b+1) <=> (a-c)(b-c) = c(c+1) = c^2+c
  PROOF: ab = c(a+b+1). Then ab-ca-cb = c.
  a(b-c) - c(b-c) = c + c^2 - c^2 = c ... hmm let me redo.
  ab - c(a+b+1) = 0 => ab - ca - cb - c = 0
  => a(b-c) = c(b+1) => (a-c)(b-c) = c(b+1) - c(b-c)
  Actually: ab = ca + cb + c => ab - ca - cb + c^2 = c^2 + c
  => (a-c)(b-c) = c^2 + c = c(c+1). QED!

  So the solutions are EXACTLY the factorizations of c(c+1):
  c(c+1) = d1 * d2 with d1 <= d2, a = c+d1, b = c+d2.

  c     c(c+1)    factorizations       (a,b) pairs
  -----------------------------------------------------------------
    1       2     1*2->2,3
    2       6     1*6->3,8; 2*3->4,5
    3      12     1*12->4,15; 2*6->5,9; 3*4->6,7
    4      20     1*20->5,24; 2*10->6,14; 4*5->8,9
    5      30     1*30->6,35; 2*15->7,20; 3*10->8,15; 5*6->10,11
    6      42     1*42->7,48; 2*21->8,27; 3*14->9,20; 6*7->12,13
    7      56     1*56->8,63; 2*28->9,35; 4*14->11,21; 7*8->14,15
    8      72     1*72->9,80; 2*36->10,44; 3*24->11,32; 4*18->12,26; 6*12->14,20; 8*9->16,17
    9      90     1*90->10,99; 2*45->11,54; 3*30->12,39; 5*18->14,27; 6*15->15,24; 9*10->18,19
   10     110     1*110->11,120; 2*55->12,65; 5*22->15,32; 10*11->20,21
   11     132     1*132->12,143; 2*66->13,77; 3*44->14,55; 4*33->15,44; 6*22->17,33; 11*12->22,23

  THE NUMBER OF COMPOSITIONS TO c IS tau(c(c+1))/2
  (half the number of divisors of c(c+1), since d1 <= d2).

  tau(c(c+1)) for c = 1,...,20:
    c= 1: c(c+1) =    2, tau =   2, compositions = 1
    c= 2: c(c+1) =    6, tau =   4, compositions = 2
    c= 3: c(c+1) =   12, tau =   6, compositions = 3
    c= 4: c(c+1) =   20, tau =   6, compositions = 3
    c= 5: c(c+1) =   30, tau =   8, compositions = 4
    c= 6: c(c+1) =   42, tau =   8, compositions = 4
    c= 7: c(c+1) =   56, tau =   8, compositions = 4
    c= 8: c(c+1) =   72, tau =  12, compositions = 6
    c= 9: c(c+1) =   90, tau =  12, compositions = 6
    c=10: c(c+1) =  110, tau =   8, compositions = 4
    c=11: c(c+1) =  132, tau =  12, compositions = 6
    c=12: c(c+1) =  156, tau =  12, compositions = 6
    c=13: c(c+1) =  182, tau =   8, compositions = 4
    c=14: c(c+1) =  210, tau =  16, compositions = 8
    c=15: c(c+1) =  240, tau =  20, compositions = 10
    c=16: c(c+1) =  272, tau =  10, compositions = 5
    c=17: c(c+1) =  306, tau =  12, compositions = 6
    c=18: c(c+1) =  342, tau =  12, compositions = 6
    c=19: c(c+1) =  380, tau =  12, compositions = 6
