  Highly composite c(c+1) give many compositions.
  c=6: 6*7=42=2*3*7, tau=8, 4 compositions.
  c=12: 12*13=156=4*3*13, tau=12, 6 compositions.

============================================================
RAPIDITY CHAINS: ITERATED COMPOSITIONS
============================================================

  Start with the octave 1/3 (c=1) and iterate:
  Can we reach all 1/(2k+1) by composing with themselves?

  1/3 (+) 1/3 = 3/5  (NOT of form 1/(2n+1), since 5 is odd but 3/5 != 1/k)
  So the octave cannot even compose with itself to stay in the set!

  1/5 (+) 1/5 = (2/5)/(1+1/25) = (2/5)/(26/25) = 50/130 = 5/13
  5/13: is this 1/(2c+1)? 13/5 = 2.6. Not integer.

  SELF-COMPOSITION 1/(2a+1) (+) 1/(2a+1):
  = 2/(2a+1) / (1 + 1/(2a+1)^2)
  = 2(2a+1) / ((2a+1)^2 + 1)
  = (4a+2) / (4a^2+4a+2)
  = (2a+1) / (2a^2+2a+1)

  For this to be 1/(2c+1), need (2a^2+2a+1)/(2a+1) = 2c+1
  => 2a^2+2a+1 = (2a+1)(2c+1) = 4ac+2a+2c+1
  => 2a^2 = 4ac+2c = 2c(2a+1)
  => c = a^2/(2a+1)
  Integer iff (2a+1) | a^2.

  gcd(a, 2a+1) = gcd(a, 1) = 1. So gcd(a^2, 2a+1) = 1.
  Therefore (2a+1) | a^2 only if 2a+1 = 1, i.e., a = 0.

  THEOREM: No musical interval can compose with itself to
  produce another musical interval. Self-composition ALWAYS
  leaves the set {1/(2n+1)}.

  This is because gcd(a^2, 2a+1) = 1: consecutive-ish
  numbers are always coprime. The PARITY obstruction
  prevents self-doubling in rapidity space.

============================================================
THE c(c+1) STRUCTURE AND PRONIC NUMBERS
============================================================

  The number of ways to compose to interval c is tau(c(c+1))/2
  where c(c+1) is a PRONIC NUMBER (product of consecutive integers).

  Pronic numbers: 2, 6, 12, 20, 30, 42, 56, 72, 90, 110, 132, ...
  = n(n+1) = 2*T_n where T_n is the n-th triangular number.

  c(c+1) is ALWAYS EVEN (one of c, c+1 is even).
  c(c+1) is NEVER a perfect square (consecutive integers can't be).

  The rapidity algebra is governed by PRONIC FACTORIZATION.
  Each factorization c(c+1) = d1*d2 gives a pair (c+d1, c+d2)
  of musical interval indices whose relativistic composition
  equals interval c.

  For the FORBIDDEN c=3 (interval 1/7):
  c(c+1) = 12 = 1*12 = 2*6 = 3*4
  Pairs: (4,15), (5,9), (6,7)
  i.e., 1/9 (+) 1/31, 1/11 (+) 1/19, 1/13 (+) 1/15 all give 1/7.
  THREE ways to reach the threshold.

  For the FORBIDDEN c=10 (interval 1/21):
  c(c+1) = 110 = 1*110 = 2*55 = 5*22 = 10*11
  Pairs: (11,120), (12,65), (15,32), (20,21)
  FOUR ways to reach the forbidden number 21.
  And 110 = 2*5*11 = the Paley primes {5, 11} times 2!

